WEBVTT

00:02.480 --> 00:04.650
<b></b>Ginza Shopping Center

00:02.480 --> 00:04.650
<b></b>Ginza M's Building

00:02.910 --> 00:06.150
What? You want... <i>me</i> for that?

00:06.150 --> 00:10.100
Yes. My grandfather absolutely insists.

00:10.100 --> 00:12.280
Mm-hmm. I'd like you to.

00:12.280 --> 00:17.790
I don't think it's my place to comment 
on who you hire at the hotel.

00:17.790 --> 00:22.670
Well, I'm not asking you to act like 
you work in the HR department.

00:23.240 --> 00:26.300
You've heard of the Cocktail Award, right?

00:27.620 --> 00:31.570
Yes. Some bartenders I know are competing.

00:31.570 --> 00:33.680
I've been hearing a lot about it.

00:34.100 --> 00:38.760
The Cocktail Award is a competition 
for original cocktails.

00:39.160 --> 00:43.020
It isn't just about demonstrating 
your abilities to the judges.

00:43.020 --> 00:47.690
It's also a test of your imagination 
in creating a new cocktail.

00:48.090 --> 00:52.850
The hotel's lounge bar is a kind of stage 
where one performs for a large audience.

00:52.850 --> 00:55.170
I'm not looking for mere skill.

00:55.170 --> 01:00.580
I want a bartender 
who's willing to try new things.

01:00.580 --> 01:03.360
Well, yes, that's about it.

01:03.360 --> 01:10.270
So we'd like to ask you to observe the 
Cocktail Award finals and share your opinions.

01:10.270 --> 01:12.720
You don't need to put 
too much thought into it.

01:12.980 --> 01:18.180
All we ask is that you tell us
 which contestants you found interesting.

01:18.180 --> 01:21.100
So, would that be all right with you?

01:22.210 --> 01:24.610
You always ask the impossible 
as if it's nothing.

01:25.420 --> 01:29.280
I'm sure this is difficult 
for the contestants too, though.

01:47.000 --> 01:53.720
<b></b>Glass of God

03:02.540 --> 03:04.200
Have a good evening.

03:02.540 --> 03:05.660
<b>Minami</b>

03:04.200 --> 03:05.660
Good work today.

03:05.910 --> 03:07.880
Are you going to stay and practice again?

03:07.880 --> 03:11.420
Yes. I'll be sure to clean up afterward.

03:11.900 --> 03:14.300
Don't wear yourself out, okay?

03:14.300 --> 03:15.930
All right.

03:18.530 --> 03:24.100
<i>That's easy to say, but I've got to practice 
a lot more to compete for the award.</i>

03:26.100 --> 03:30.190
<i>I've got to make a cocktail 
in front of all of those judges,</i>

03:30.750 --> 03:33.300
<i>so I'd better be confident 
about what I'm doing.</i>

03:34.040 --> 03:35.070
Ouch.

03:36.910 --> 03:42.090
<i>If they see how clumsy I am, it'll tarnish 
the reputation of Bar Minami.</i>

03:43.920 --> 03:47.030
<i>Anyway, it's all about practice!</i>

03:48.050 --> 03:51.890
So, how's it going? Decide on a recipe yet?

03:51.890 --> 03:53.210
Not yet.

03:53.210 --> 03:55.970
Oh? Are those past winners?

03:55.970 --> 03:58.070
I thought they might give me a hint.

03:58.070 --> 04:01.340
The award is a competition 
for original cocktails.

04:01.340 --> 04:04.350
That "original" part is 
the real tricky part, huh?

04:04.700 --> 04:09.400
Yes. Every year, many 
new recipes are submitted.

04:09.400 --> 04:12.610
I have to make something different 
from all of them. A real original.

04:12.920 --> 04:17.270
I guess, historically speaking, 
the most famous one was Snow Country.

04:17.270 --> 04:18.610
It seems so.

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b>m 0 0 l 17 0 13 5 0 5</b>

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b>m 0 0 l 100 0 100 70 0 70</b>

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b></b>RECIPE

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b>Snow Country</b>

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b></b>Yukiguni

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b></b>Japanese Craft Vodka HAKU (White)

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b></b>Hermes White Curaçao

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b></b>Suntory Cocktail Lime

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b></b>1. Shake, then pour into a cocktail glass that
has been rimmed snow-style with sugar.

04:18.610 --> 04:22.120
<b></b>2. Decorate with a mint cherry.

04:19.260 --> 04:22.120
1959, huh.

04:22.650 --> 04:24.530
How old were you then?

04:24.530 --> 04:29.150
Oh, I wasn't born yet. That was even 
before Okinawa was returned to Japan.

04:29.150 --> 04:31.490
Is that right? What a surprise.

04:31.490 --> 04:32.750
What's a surprise?

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b></b>1. Shake and pour into cocktail glass.

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b></b>2. Garnish with candy, heavenly bamboo leaf,
glass noodle, dragée, black olive.

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b>Umemiyabi</b>

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b>m 0 0 l 17 0 13 5 0 5</b>

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b>m 0 0 l 100 0 100 75 0 75</b>

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b></b>RECIPE

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b></b>Suntory Plum Liqueur 20ml
(Blended with Sakura Barrel-Aged Plum Liqueur
 from Yamazaki Distillery)

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b></b>Lejay Crème de Cassis 15ml

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b></b>Calvados Boulard Grand Solage 10ml

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b></b>Fresh Orange Juice 10ml

04:32.750 --> 04:43.510
<b></b>Sandeman Ruby Porto 5ml

04:33.070 --> 04:34.130
What's that one?

04:34.130 --> 04:37.450
The cocktail that won last year. 
The Umemiyabi.

04:37.450 --> 04:42.850
"The plum wine-based Japanese craft cocktail 
that enchanted the world," huh.

04:43.510 --> 04:46.730
People sure think up 
some interesting drinks.

04:46.730 --> 04:52.270
The recipe is hard enough, but the part 
I'm struggling with most is the name.

04:52.760 --> 04:56.290
It has to be memorable, 
easy to order at a bar,

04:56.290 --> 04:58.530
and have some kind of story behind it.

04:58.830 --> 05:01.870
You always have an eye 
for details like that.

05:01.870 --> 05:03.480
Don't you think <i>eye</i>'m right?

05:03.480 --> 05:07.290
If I could just figure out the name, 
everything else would fall into place.

05:07.700 --> 05:12.740
Well, don't overthink it, or else 
you're gonna make your chiburu yamu.

05:12.740 --> 05:17.050
Nobody says "head hurts" 
in the Okinawan dialect anymore.

05:17.050 --> 05:19.550
Nankuru nai! It'll all work out!

05:19.980 --> 05:22.580
Okinawan dialect, huh...

05:35.810 --> 05:37.820
<b>Ryukyu Aged</b>

05:35.810 --> 05:37.820
<b>Shimakiku</b>

05:35.810 --> 05:37.820
<b></b>Ryukyu Awamori
30 Percent

05:35.810 --> 05:37.820
<b>Oukyu</b>

05:35.810 --> 05:37.820
Ryukyu
Awamori

05:35.810 --> 05:37.820
<b>Umikaze</b>

05:35.810 --> 05:37.820
<b>Imaizumi</b>

05:46.330 --> 05:48.330
<b><b>Ogura</b></b>

05:52.330 --> 05:54.330
<b>Sharpen
Up</b>

05:59.710 --> 06:01.840
<b>Minami</b>

06:00.100 --> 06:01.840
Have a good evening.

06:06.400 --> 06:07.510
Good work today.

06:07.510 --> 06:09.600
Oh, Minami-san!

06:09.600 --> 06:13.600
I'm sorry. I start to worry 
when I'm not practicing.

06:14.150 --> 06:19.980
Two things that are crucial for a 
bartender are to see and to be seen.

06:20.630 --> 06:25.230
Your customers are also watching you, 
just like the judges will.

06:25.230 --> 06:27.790
You mustn't make your guests 
feel uncomfortable.

06:28.400 --> 06:29.620
Right.

06:29.890 --> 06:35.020
Why don't you actually take a look 
at yourself? To see how you look?

06:35.020 --> 06:35.890
Huh?

06:38.040 --> 06:39.130
<i>Good idea.</i>

06:39.400 --> 06:44.340
<i>If I watch a video of myself, I may spot 
some mistakes and things I need to change.</i>

06:47.360 --> 06:50.010
<i>I shouldn't get so nervous recording myself.</i>

06:53.670 --> 06:55.890
So you already went to the semi-finals?

06:55.890 --> 06:58.370
Yes. Last weekend.

06:58.370 --> 06:59.640
How did it go?

06:59.640 --> 07:02.150
I think it went well.

07:02.480 --> 07:05.400
Now I just have to wait for 
the judges' decision.

07:05.850 --> 07:08.730
While I continue practicing, of course.

07:08.730 --> 07:12.910
I've been shaking that big shaker 
so much that I built some muscle!

07:12.910 --> 07:15.100
That must have been really tough.

07:15.100 --> 07:19.370
Oh, that's right. 
He was one of the contestants!

07:19.370 --> 07:20.170
Huh?

07:20.170 --> 07:24.820
Chen-kun! Chen-kun. I was so surprised.

07:25.450 --> 07:26.680
Chen-kun was there?

07:26.680 --> 07:32.930
Yes. I heard that he quit Bar K, but I wonder 
if he's been training at another bar.

07:32.930 --> 07:35.460
How did he seem then?

07:35.460 --> 07:39.190
Well, he was unfriendly, as usual,

07:39.480 --> 07:41.820
but what really frustrated me was that...

07:45.510 --> 07:46.950
he was still so hot.

07:49.780 --> 07:53.080
Sasakura-san, have you heard anything?

07:53.080 --> 07:57.580
No. I haven't heard anything 
since the meeting with his father.

07:58.250 --> 08:01.460
But I guess he didn't give up 
on being a bartender.

08:14.790 --> 08:19.660
I'm so glad that everybody 
made it into the finals!

08:19.660 --> 08:26.050
So am I. They've spent every waking moment 
practicing for their big performance.

08:26.050 --> 08:28.110
I hope everything goes well.

08:38.860 --> 08:40.750
Haisai, gang!

08:42.170 --> 08:45.750
I brought something for you all to eat.

08:45.750 --> 08:47.570
Sata andagi.

08:48.130 --> 08:50.460
Try to keep your composure.

08:50.460 --> 08:54.510
Don't get so flustered you 
can't speak a <i>ward</i>. Yeah.

08:54.880 --> 08:56.770
Just go grab that <i>award</i>.

08:57.900 --> 09:00.640
What was that? Some kind of dad joke?

09:01.370 --> 09:02.710
Yuri-chan.

09:03.760 --> 09:05.090
Chibariyo!

09:06.260 --> 09:07.260
I will!

09:14.660 --> 09:17.960
I want to live my life as a bartender!

09:22.110 --> 09:24.680
<i>If Dad doesn't want to allow it,</i>

09:29.160 --> 09:31.580
<i>then I'll have to force him 
to accept the fact</i>

09:33.120 --> 09:37.110
<i>that I'm a bartender!</i>

09:45.110 --> 09:48.110
<b></b>Glass of God {神のグラス}

09:49.580 --> 09:53.490
<i>Ladies and gentlemen, 
thank you for joining us today.</i>

09:53.830 --> 09:59.490
<i>It's time for the Suntory 
Cocktail Award finals to begin.</i>

09:59.830 --> 10:03.140
<i>But first, we would like to ask 
the representative from the judges,</i>

10:03.140 --> 10:06.530
<i>Kuzuhara Ryuichi, to say a few words.</i>

10:07.890 --> 10:15.010
In 1931, The Cocktail Award was established 
as the first cocktail competition in Japan.

10:15.010 --> 10:19.510
It was held to facilitate the spread 
of Western cocktail culture in Japan.

10:19.850 --> 10:24.650
What we ask of you bartenders
 is your unfaltering discipline

10:24.650 --> 10:27.610
and creative innovation.

10:27.610 --> 10:31.650
We hope that each of you can fully 
demonstrate the fruits of your labor today

10:31.650 --> 10:33.700
and wish you the best of luck.

10:35.560 --> 10:41.040
<i>Well then, on to entry 
number one: Kinjo Yuri.</i>

11:11.620 --> 11:14.950
<i>And now, please present your cocktail.</i>

11:15.980 --> 11:19.450
This cocktail is called the Chimugukuru.

11:17.320 --> 11:24.830
<b>Chimugukuru</b>

11:17.320 --> 11:24.830
<b>m 0 0 l 80 0 80 60 0 60</b>

11:17.320 --> 11:24.830
<b></b>Japanese Craft Vodka 30ml

11:17.320 --> 11:24.830
<b></b>Lejay Pear 10ml

11:17.320 --> 11:24.830
<b></b>Hermes Blue Curaçao 10ml

11:17.320 --> 11:24.830
<b></b>Suntory Sudachi Watsunagi 5ml

11:17.320 --> 11:24.830
<b></b>Fresh Lime Juice 5ml

11:19.450 --> 11:24.830
When I left my home in Okinawa and was 
struggling to fit into a new environment,

11:24.830 --> 11:28.800
my bartending senpai, who was also 
from Okinawa, came to my rescue.

11:29.870 --> 11:34.510
The name is an Okinawan word 
that means "heart" or "empathy."

11:34.510 --> 11:38.920
The drink expresses the warmth 
of friendship with a vodka that,

11:38.920 --> 11:41.720
like Okinawan Awamori, 
is made from rice. Haku.

11:41.720 --> 11:45.570
It is also made to be 
reminiscent of the blue sea.

11:46.780 --> 11:48.580
<i>Thank you very much.</i>

11:53.030 --> 11:57.960
<i>Next up is entry number two: Kawakami Kyoko.</i>

11:59.310 --> 12:01.110
<i>The glasses are chilled.</i>

12:01.110 --> 12:06.250
<i>The positioning of the shaker, 
measuring cup, and bottles is okay.</i>

12:06.250 --> 12:08.530
<i>Now I just have to do what I practiced.</i>

12:22.300 --> 12:25.420
<i>Why... can't I move?</i>

12:26.410 --> 12:28.530
<i>I practiced so much for this.</i>

12:30.090 --> 12:32.040
<i>I'm gonna run out of time.</i>

12:33.290 --> 12:37.200
<i>If I fail now, it'll look bad for Bar Minami!</i>

12:40.960 --> 12:44.730
<i>Minami-san... came to root for me?</i>

12:48.410 --> 12:53.680
<i>He's the one who sent me to the 
competition to represent his bar.</i>

12:54.130 --> 12:56.420
<i>He believes in me!</i>

13:18.120 --> 13:21.430
My cocktail is called the Headwind.

13:19.820 --> 13:27.830
<b>m 0 0 l 80 0 80 60 0 60</b>

13:19.820 --> 13:27.830
<b>Headwind</b>

13:19.820 --> 13:27.830
<b>(Mukaikaze)</b>

13:19.820 --> 13:27.830
<b></b>Suntory World Whiskey Ao 30ml

13:19.820 --> 13:27.830
<b></b>Kimia 10ml

13:19.820 --> 13:27.830
<b></b>Drambuie 10ml

13:19.820 --> 13:27.830
<b></b>Suntory Ginger Watsunagi  5ml

13:19.820 --> 13:27.830
<b></b>Fresh Lemon Juice 5ml

13:21.920 --> 13:27.830
Since deciding to become a bartender, 
I've faced a lot of challenges and setbacks.

13:27.830 --> 13:33.650
I decided to to face that wind of pain 
and confusion head on and inhale all of it!

13:33.650 --> 13:35.340
That's why I made this drink.

13:35.340 --> 13:37.820
The flavor has a big kick to it.

13:38.180 --> 13:40.470
But don't give up. Face it head on!

13:53.130 --> 13:58.910
<i>Next up is entry number 12: Kelvin Chen.</i>

13:54.480 --> 13:57.110
<b></b>Kelvin Chen

14:05.990 --> 14:08.160
His movements really are graceful.

14:08.160 --> 14:10.370
Yeah. But...

14:16.480 --> 14:20.730
<i>I thoroughly researched cocktail contests 
around the world, not only those in Japan,</i>

14:20.730 --> 14:23.380
<i>and studied what points 
they were evaluated on.</i>

14:23.730 --> 14:27.930
<i>Movements have to be big, but smooth. 
Use your whole hand, down to the fingertips.</i>

14:29.120 --> 14:32.990
<i>That said, I never expected 
Kuzuhara-sensei to be one of the judges,</i>

14:33.520 --> 14:36.180
<i>but he isn't the type of person to let 
personal feelings cloud his judgment.</i>

14:36.620 --> 14:38.060
<i>There's no need to hesitate.</i>

14:39.030 --> 14:41.880
<i>I'll serve an equal amount with each pour.</i>

14:44.610 --> 14:50.240
<i>I sent my dad the address for the online 
video stream. This is my response to him.</i>

14:50.910 --> 14:52.840
<i>Now they'll all have to accept me.</i>

14:52.840 --> 14:55.730
<i>The judges... as well as my dad!</i>

15:08.150 --> 15:10.310
<i>I've failed?</i>

15:10.950 --> 15:14.680
<i>I can't expect a decent result now.</i>

15:15.220 --> 15:19.840
<i>Is this the end of my life as a bartender?</i>

15:27.320 --> 15:28.300
Huh?

15:30.770 --> 15:32.210
Why?

15:33.610 --> 15:36.210
I named this Lion City.

15:36.210 --> 15:42.210
<b>m 0 0 l 80 0 80 65 0 65</b>

15:36.210 --> 15:42.210
<b>Lion City</b>

15:36.210 --> 15:42.210
<b></b>Japanese Craft Gin 20ml

15:36.210 --> 15:42.210
<b></b>Lejay Framboise 20ml

15:36.210 --> 15:42.210
<b></b>Suntory Yuzu Watsunagi 10ml

15:36.210 --> 15:42.210
<b></b>Suntory Grenadine Syrup 10ml

15:36.210 --> 15:42.210
<b></b>Fresh Pineapple Juice 20ml

15:36.210 --> 15:42.210
<b></b>Fresh Lemon Juice 10ml

15:36.800 --> 15:41.520
I wanted to make a cocktail that would 
represent my home, Singapore.

15:43.310 --> 15:44.840
May I ask a question?

15:44.840 --> 15:48.690
Why did you only finish one cocktail?

15:48.690 --> 15:54.850
Even if you couldn't make the required five 
cocktails, you at least could have made four.

15:56.040 --> 16:04.990
I decided to participate in the award 
because I wanted to be praised and accepted.

16:05.980 --> 16:07.800
But I realized that, for a very long time,

16:07.800 --> 16:11.620
all I've cared about 
is being accepted by others.

16:12.130 --> 16:14.470
But that wasn't because 
I wanted to improve myself.

16:15.400 --> 16:19.100
I think I was simply afraid 
of not being accepted.

16:20.130 --> 16:27.260
However, after I made that mistake, I still 
strongly felt that I didn't want to give up.

16:28.200 --> 16:33.010
Even if I am disqualified for this, I wanted 
to complete the cocktail that I created.

16:33.780 --> 16:37.770
I put all of those feelings 
into this one glass.

16:38.200 --> 16:43.360
Well then, why did you use a recipe 
different from the one you submitted?

16:43.900 --> 16:47.660
That recipe shows only one chrysanthemum,

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b>Kelvin Chen</b>

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b>Lion City</b>

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b>m 0 0 l 80 0 80 65 0 65</b>

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b></b>Japanese Craft Gin 20ml

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b></b>Lejay Framboise 20ml

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b></b>Suntory Yuzu Watsunagi 10ml

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b></b>Suntory Grenadine Syrup 10ml

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b></b>Fresh Pineapple Juice 20ml

16:43.900 --> 16:48.650
<b></b>Fresh Lemon Juice 10ml

16:47.660 --> 16:51.780
but your final drink is garnished with two.

16:52.190 --> 16:56.270
I made this cocktail to represent Singapore.

16:56.270 --> 17:00.420
However, I then realized that wasn't enough.

17:01.620 --> 17:03.850
I don't have one home country.

17:04.350 --> 17:09.420
I also have roots in the country 
with a flag of red and white. Japan.

17:09.880 --> 17:11.890
In addition to my pride in my fatherland,

17:12.240 --> 17:15.120
I want to show my respect to the country 
that taught me how to live as a bartender.

17:15.120 --> 17:17.930
To the culture of Japan.

17:17.930 --> 17:22.830
And this is the cocktail I wanted 
to make. This is my cocktail.

17:23.900 --> 17:25.970
I have no further questions.

17:27.780 --> 17:29.160
Chen-san!

17:34.550 --> 17:37.830
<i>Well then, it is time to present the winner.</i>

17:43.160 --> 17:46.840
Keypad

17:43.160 --> 17:46.840
Speaker

17:43.160 --> 17:46.840
Others

17:44.890 --> 17:45.900
Hello?

17:57.930 --> 17:59.930
<b>Chimugukuru</b>

17:57.930 --> 17:59.930
<b>Kinjo Yuri</b>

17:57.930 --> 17:59.930
<b>Congratulations! 
Winner</b>

18:00.340 --> 18:03.190
Yuri-chan, one more Chimugukuru.

18:03.190 --> 18:04.580
Coming up.

18:08.430 --> 18:11.950
I wonder what happened 
to Chen-san after that.

18:12.260 --> 18:15.890
I hear he's continuing to train 
as a bartender in Singapore

18:16.440 --> 18:18.450
at the bar in his father's hotel.

18:18.450 --> 18:21.960
Really? I'm glad.

18:22.320 --> 18:25.960
Have you decided on a bartender 
to work in your lounge yet?

18:26.450 --> 18:31.970
Yes. Following your recommendation, 
we made an offer to Kyoko-san.

18:31.970 --> 18:38.300
My grandfather was happily saying, "I bet
she'll put a new wind in this bar's sails."

18:38.300 --> 18:39.520
I'm glad to hear it.

18:42.730 --> 18:45.100
<b>Minami</b>

18:43.790 --> 18:45.100
Kawakami-kun.

18:45.100 --> 18:46.230
Yes?

18:46.230 --> 18:49.550
I'm going out for a bit. 
Keep an eye on things.

18:49.550 --> 18:50.860
All right.

18:54.680 --> 18:58.410
It's been four years 
since I first came to this bar.

19:01.200 --> 19:05.120
Oh, shove it! I'm not drunk at all!

19:05.120 --> 19:09.770
<i>I drowned my sorrows a little too much 
and was an awful customer.</i>

19:11.670 --> 19:16.380
Do you know what kind of job 
bartending really is?

19:16.990 --> 19:21.100
It's to make sure that 
when a guest goes home,

19:21.100 --> 19:26.140
they're just a little happier than 
when they walked through the door.

19:26.460 --> 19:28.720
That's what this work is for.

19:30.050 --> 19:34.310
As such, perhaps it's best to 
make that your last drink.

19:37.560 --> 19:42.160
And now I'm standing here behind the bar.

19:44.320 --> 19:45.700
Welcome.

19:45.700 --> 19:48.640
Uh. Minami-san?

19:48.640 --> 19:51.550
I came alone. Is that all right?

19:52.790 --> 19:55.070
I'd like a Horse's Neck.

19:56.410 --> 19:58.800
Uh, sure. As you wish.

20:03.170 --> 20:06.690
<i>Could this possibly mean...?</i>

20:08.240 --> 20:10.910
Here you are. A Horse's Neck.

20:23.580 --> 20:24.870
Um...

20:24.870 --> 20:29.190
I, Kawakami Kyoko, would like 
to move on from Bar Minami.

20:29.190 --> 20:30.580
Is that all right?

20:31.260 --> 20:34.840
A bartender never moves on.

20:35.280 --> 20:39.360
From day to day, from second to second, 
a person is always changing.

20:39.360 --> 20:44.090
The cocktails you mix for guests 
should also always be changing.

20:44.680 --> 20:49.860
Bartending is a job with 
a beginning but no end.

20:50.520 --> 20:56.230
Thank you very much. I wish you 
the best of luck at your new position.

20:56.230 --> 20:57.740
Sir!

20:58.740 --> 21:06.410
Was I able to make you just a little happier 
than when you walked through the door?

21:08.850 --> 21:09.940
Yes.

21:14.670 --> 21:16.730
Thank you very much.

22:49.470 --> 22:53.180
So, how did things go 
with that fellow from Singapore?

22:53.180 --> 22:57.190
He was extremely immature. Very green.

22:57.190 --> 23:00.070
That sounds like he has promise.

23:00.410 --> 23:03.690
The Western cocktail was 
originally an imported culture,

23:03.690 --> 23:06.700
but Japanese bars made 
their own new developments,

23:06.700 --> 23:09.940
and now young people come 
from overseas to study that.

23:09.940 --> 23:12.370
How the times have changed.

23:13.650 --> 23:19.460
Speaking of green, do you still have 
your sights set on that one bartender?

23:19.860 --> 23:25.370
There's nobody else worth
opening up the counter bar for.

23:26.040 --> 23:27.970
Do you think he'll eventually 
make his way there?

23:27.970 --> 23:30.980
With the Glass of God?

23:30.980 --> 23:34.150
We're certainly making progress toward that.

23:34.550 --> 23:38.630
But he is moving very slowly.

23:39.310 --> 23:41.320
We apologize for the 
inconvenience caused by construction. 
We are conscious of the local 
environment and are
taking pains to work as safely
 as possible, so we hope you 
can please forgive this temporary intrusion.

